00:07:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. There’s a reason that stereotype exists—many of us have seen a family gathering turn sloppy because a younger crowd didn’t know where the off-switch was. So your worry isn’t irrational; it’s pattern recognition. The upside? You’re not dealing with a random house party. This is a family dinner with a host who, at 22, likely wants to be taken seriously as an adult. That desire to prove maturity is a powerful lever. The trick is to treat the drinking concern not as a “teenage problem” but as part of the hosting responsibility you can help them get right. Here’s a more direct, no-nonsense way to handle it: **1. Name the elephant without attacking** Have a short, private, non-accusatory conversation. Something like: *“I’m really proud of you for hosting. I’ll be straight with you—we both know that with our family mix, if the drinks flow too freely, things can slide into messy territory fast. I don’t want you to have that memory attached to your first dinner. Let’s figure out a few guardrails so you come out looking like a champ.”* This acknowledges the reality without painting your cousin as the problem. It frames it as a shared risk unrelated to their character. **2. Set hard, invisible limits** Don’t rely on people’s self-control. Control the environment: - **Buy less than you think you need.** If you run out, you run out—nobody will die. Not having a third case of beer or a second bottle of vodka is the simplest way to stop overdrinking. - **No open alcohol after dinner clean-up.** Once dessert plates are cleared, discreetly put all bottles and open containers away. Put out coffee, tea, and a single non-alcoholic dessert drink. The visual cue that “the bar is closed” works wonders. - **Use small glassware.** Even for wine, smaller pours slow people down and make it harder to accidentally drink too much without realizing. **3. Be the pace-setter yourself, visibly** If you’re a respected older family member, your behavior sets the thermostat. Stay noticeably moderate. Hold a non-alcoholic drink in your hand during pre-dinner mingling. When you do drink, sip slowly. Others in your age group will unconsciously mirror you, and the younger ones will see that the adults aren’t pounding drinks, which signals what’s expected. **4. Assign a low-key “bouncer” (yes, really)** If there’s a particular person you’re worried about—someone with a history of going too hard—have a quiet word with a trusted family member who’s willing to keep an eye on them. It’s not about babysitting, but having someone ready to gently intervene (“Hey, let me grab you a water, you’ll thank me later”) before it gets embarrassing. This often stops a single person from cranking up the whole group’s energy. **5. Don’t be afraid to be the “bad guy” if needed** If you see someone heading for a problem and the host is too busy or too polite, give yourself permission to step in—calmly, firmly, and without making a scene. A quiet “I think you’ll want to slow down so you can enjoy the rest of the evening” is better than hoping it fixes itself. Your cousin will likely be relieved someone else took the reins. Your concern isn’t about being a killjoy; it’s about protecting your cousin’s reputation and the family’s comfort. When you frame it that way, you can be a lot more hands-on without causing resentment. Good luck—and remember, even if a few people overserve themselves despite your efforts, a graceful, discreet mop-up (cab ride home, some water, no gossip) will still be counted as a win.
